  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  The warmest temperatures continue today and Monday. There is an\n   elevated heat risk for those sensitive to heat, especially\n   those without cooling or hydration.\n\n-  Low relative humidity and strong winds will result in elevated\n   fire weather concerns today in southwest Montana.\n\n-  A surge of monsoon moisture will bring clouds, showers, and\n   thunderstorms late Monday into Tuesday.\n\nA ridge of high pressure positioned just east of the Northern\nRockies remains the dominant weather feature this afternoon. The\nridge is maintaining dry and hot conditions throughout the region.\nHigh temperatures today (Sunday) and Monday will climb into the\nupper 80s to mid-90s in western Montana, while the lower valleys\nof north-central Idaho will approach 100 degrees. The position of\nthe ridge is keeping a fast-moving band of air aloft over the\nnorthwestern U.S., resulting in strong, gusty winds reaching the\nground. The combination of strong winds, high temperatures, and\nrelative humidity dropping into the single digits will result in\ncritical fire weather conditions for southwest Montana and Lemhi\nCounty. \n\nAn incoming upper-level weather disturbance, coupled with the\nleading edge of monsoonal moisture, will bring mostly cloudy\nconditions and a few showers and isolated thunderstorms Monday\nafternoon and evening. Currently, most of the showers and storms\nare expected east of the Highway 93 corridor and over the\nButte/Blackfoot Region in southwest Montana and Lemhi County in\nIdaho. Due to the very dry air at the surface, these showers and\nthunderstorms will produce strong, erratic wind gusts potentially\nexceeding 40 mph.\n\nBy Tuesday afternoon and evening, the atmosphere will moisten\nenough for more rain to reach the ground. However, the erratic\nwind gusts will remain. The shower and thunderstorm coverage will\nbecome much more widespread, covering much of western Montana. The\nStorm Prediction Center has placed portions of western Montana,\nincluding Lemhi County, under a marginal risk for severe\nthunderstorms due to the potential for strong, gusty winds. Areas\nwith the highest risk will be locations along I-90 and east of\nHighway 93 in west-central and southwest Montana, as well as Lemhi\nCounty.\n\nThe threat for severe thunderstorms increases on Wednesday and\nThursday. If you have outdoor plans through mid-week, please plan\naccordingly.",
